  "dom_id": "step-q343323",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14211452/assets/2b0876986e02941b588bf9bacbc5a0caa61eb2b95021fa4ed52408dd4f5d3f72.png)\n\nA rectangular block of clay is being reshaped as it moves through a press. Its height is decreasing at a rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/min},$ its width is increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{ ft/min},$ and its length is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{ft}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ in width, and $3\\,\\text{ft}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=10\\text{ ft},\\, & W=4\\text{ ft}, \\\\ H=3\\text{ ft},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ ft/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ ft/min},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5\\text{ ft/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(4)(3)(-2)+(10)(3)(1)+(10)(4)(-0.5) \\\\  & =-14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of $14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$",
